        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> Well, yes, it is a bit subjective. The 'mini.base16' takes similar approach on what you describe. However, during work on 'mini.hues' I took into account pointers from #240. That is, use background to differentiate diff parts so as to see the code highlighting. I think I like it better.\r\n> \r\n> Besides, they can be customized manually. Granted, not quite easy with 'randomhue', but possible. For example, by linking to `DiagnosticOk`, `DiagnosticWarn`, and `DiagnosticError`.\r\n> \r\n> Thanks again for the suggestion, but I am going to close this as not planned.\r\n\r\nFair enough - That said, would you be open to having an option to use either base16-style diff highlight or 'tokyonight'-ish style? This would make the transition from base16 to randomhue less jarring overall\r\n ",
